<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bihać</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Bihać is a city in north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bosnia">Bosnia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herzegovina">Herzegovina</a> on the Una river, near the Croatian border. A royal free town of medieval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croatia">Croatia</a>, it fell to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> in 1592. In 1942 it hosted the first session of the Yugoslav partisans' council <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anti-fascist-council-for-the-national-liberation-of-yugoslavia">AVNOJ</a>, and in the 1990s it was besieged as the Bihać pocket.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bihac.jpg" alt="Bihać" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Julian Nyča · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Biha%C4%87_%E2%80%93_Kapetanova_kula.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Bihać stands on the banks of the river Una in northwestern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bosnia">Bosnia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herzegovina">Herzegovina</a>, in the Bosanska Krajina region close to the border with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croatia">Croatia</a>. It is the administrative centre of Una-Sana Canton in the Federation of Bosnia and Herzegovina. The town was first built on the river's Island of St. Ladislav, and it was the centre of the Pounje, the lands along the Una. Its municipality includes many villages, among them Ripač, Kulen Vakuf and Brekovica.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Medieval towns around the Una began to appear in the middle of the 13th century. Bihać is first mentioned on 26 February 1260 in a charter of King Béla IV, which describes it as a town on the Island of St. Ladislav owned by the Benedictine abbey of Topusko. Two years later, in 1262, Béla proclaimed it a royal free city under the direct authority of the throne, with rights that let it develop independently of local lords. A charter of 1271 confirms the status. At the head of the municipality stood a town elder, often called a judge, and the town had a curia of magistrates and notaries who kept court and civil records.</p><p>In 1530 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> sent troops to defend seven key strongholds in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croatia">Croatia</a>, among them Bihać. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> occupied it in 1592 after a siege of ten days, and until the 19th century it was one of the most important forts in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bosnia">Bosnia</a>. Auguste Marmont, governor-general of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/illyrian-provinces">Illyrian Provinces</a>, briefly interrupted Ottoman rule on 5 May 1810 and then withdrew. Ottoman rule ended de facto after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congress-of-berlin">Congress of Berlin</a>.</p><p>During <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">World War II</a> the town was occupied by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/axis-powers">Axis</a> troops and included in the Independent State of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croatia">Croatia</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ustase">Ustaše</a> regime committed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genocide">genocide</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbs">Serbs</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holocaust">Holocaust</a>; from July to September 1941 the Ustaše killed some 15,000 Serbs, along with some <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romani-people">Roma</a>, at Garavice, near Bihać. For two months in late 1942 and early 1943 Bihać was the capital of a short-lived territory, the Bihać Republic, until German forces recaptured it. It returned to Bosnian territory on 28 March 1945. The town was besieged for three years from 1992 to 1995 during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bosnian-war">Bosnian War</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>At the 2013 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> the city had 56,261 inhabitants, of whom 88 per cent were recorded as followers of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>. The 1991 census recorded 46,737 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bosniaks">Bosniaks</a>, 12,689 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbs">Serbs</a>, 5,580 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croats">Croats</a> and 4,356 Yugoslavs in the municipality. The fertile soil makes agriculture significant for its economy.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Bihać” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Its political body, the Anti-Fascist Council for the National Liberation of Yugoslavia (AVNOJ), first met at <b>Bihać</b> on 26 November 1942.</blockquote><a href="/empire/socialist-federal-republic-of-yugoslavia">Socialist Federal Republic of Yugoslavia · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The Iapydes formed in the hinterland of the northern Adriatic during the Iron Age, around the 6th century BCE, in the Lika plateau and the Una valley near <b>Bihać</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/iapydes">Iapydes</a></li></ul></section>
